{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If the anointed priest sins to the guilt of the people.</b> That is, an inadvertent sin should not befall him except through the stumbling blocks of the people, as they said (Berakhot 34b): one who prays and errs — it is a bad sign for him; and if he is a public emissary, it is a bad sign for those who sent him. His offering is burned entire and the priest has no share in it at all; therefore Scripture did not write concerning it וְאָשֵׁם (\"and he is guilty\") as it wrote concerning all other sinners — for saying וְאָשֵׁם indeed conveys an admonition toward repentance, and this does not apply to the anointed priest, since the sin did not come from his own heart at all, but befell him through the guilt of the people. And in the case of an inadvertent ruling of the Sanhedrin, which is also remote [from the heart], he [Scripture] likewise said it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The final clause וּבְשִׁגְגַת סַנְהֶדְרִין שֶׁהוּא גַם כֵּן רָחוֹק אָמַר is elliptical; read as: Scripture likewise used וְאָשֵׁם in the case of the Sanhedrin's inadvertent error (Lev 4:13), which is also remote from the heart."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:35:46.441063653Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}